\chapter{Relativistic Mass Conversion in the SME-ASTRO-v10.0 and Matter Jet Mk-30}

\section{Overview}
Conventional engines consume matter through chemical or nuclear processes that
rearrange or partially convert mass. In contrast, the Sovereign Matter Engine
SME-ASTRO-v10.0 operates in a regime where matter is not merely consumed but
\emph{converted} into energy through relativistic mass defect and high-energy
plasma interactions. This chapter formalizes the physics of mass conversion,
distinguishing it from chemical consumption and nuclear burn-up, and establishes
its role in the Matter Jet Mk-30's 10 PW power envelope.

\section{Mass Conversion vs. Mass Consumption}
Chemical combustion preserves total mass, while nuclear reactions convert only a
small fraction of mass into energy. Relativistic engines, however, operate in a
domain where matter is transformed directly into energy through:
\begin{itemize}
\item relativistic kinetic energy amplification,
\item baryonic mass defect,
\item gamma-ray emission channels,
\item annihilation-driven photon production.
\end{itemize}

In this regime, matter does not remain in a conserved chemical or nuclear form.
Instead, it is removed from the mass ledger entirely.

\section{Relativistic Mass Defect}
The mass defect associated with the Mk-30's 10 PW power envelope is given by:
\begin{equation}
\Delta m = \frac{P_0}{c^2}.
\end{equation}

For $P_0 = 1.0 \times 10^{16}$ W:
\begin{equation}
\Delta m = 1.11 \times 10^{-1}~\text{g/s}.
\end{equation}

This mass is not consumed chemically; it is \emph{converted} into energy and
radiated primarily as gamma photons and relativistic plasma kinetic energy.

\section{Baryonic Carrier Flux}
The baryonic injection rate of the Mk-30 is:
\begin{equation}
\dot{m}_{\text{carrier}} = 12.98~\text{g/s}.
\end{equation}

This mass is not fully annihilated. Instead, it serves as a plasma carrier
medium whose relativistic acceleration produces the mass defect. The carrier
mass remains largely intact, while a small fraction undergoes conversion.

\section{Energy Conversion Efficiency}
The Mk-30 operates at:
\begin{equation}
\eta = 0.95,
\end{equation}
meaning 95\% of the injected energy contributes to jet formation, while 5\% is
dissipated as waste heat:
\begin{equation}
Q_{\text{waste}} = (1 - \eta) P_0 = 5.0 \times 10^{14}~\text{W}.
\end{equation}

This waste heat is managed through GaIn micro-channel cooling and CNT radiators.

\section{Gamma-Ray Production}
Mass conversion produces gamma photons through:
\begin{itemize}
\item electron-positron annihilation,
\item Bremsstrahlung radiation,
\item synchrotron emission in kilotesla fields.
\end{itemize}

The characteristic annihilation peak occurs at:
\begin{equation}
E_{\gamma} = 511~\text{keV}.
\end{equation}

High-purity germanium detectors confirm this signature.

\section{Physical Implications}
Mass conversion implies:
\begin{itemize}
\item matter is not consumed but transformed,
\item energy output exceeds chemical or nuclear limits,
\item relativistic jets require only minimal mass input,
\item the Mk-30 operates in a domain beyond classical propulsion.
\end{itemize}

This establishes the SME-ASTRO-v10.0 as a physically feasible relativistic
engine capable of sustained 10 PW operation.

\section{Conclusion}
Mass conversion is the defining physical mechanism of the SME-ASTRO-v10.0 and
Matter Jet Mk-30. Unlike chemical or nuclear engines, the Mk-30 transforms
matter directly into energy through relativistic mass defect and high-energy
plasma interactions. This chapter formalizes the physics underlying this
process, demonstrating its consistency with conservation laws and its necessity
for petawatt-scale relativistic propulsion.

